<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bosphorus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bosphorus is a narrow strait in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> connecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> and separating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> stood on its European shore, controlling traffic between the two seas. This strategic position underlay the foundation and long survival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>'s capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bosphorus.jpg" alt="Bosphorus" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Interiot · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkish_Strait_disambig.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…on 11 May 330 CE, on the site of the ancient Greek colony of Byzantion at the strategic crossing of the <b>Bosphorus</b>, is the conventional founding moment of what later scholars called the Byzantine Empire, though its inhabitants called themselves Romans and recognised no break in Roman continuity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…murder as a pretext for the most ambitious Sasanian offensive in centuries: from 603 his armies overran Armenia, Mesopotamia, Syria and Palestine, conquered Egypt by around 621, and twice reached the <b>Bosphorus</b> opposite Constantinople, culminating in the siege of 626, achieving an extent that rivalled the Achaemenid empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>A Rus' fleet of about 200 vessels sailed into the <b>Bosphorus</b> and began pillaging the suburbs of Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-18">18 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Yamaks refusing the uniforms of the Nizamı Cedit killed Raif Mehmet, the minister of the <b>Bosphorus</b>, and marched on Constantinople under Kabakçı Mustafa.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-25">25 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
